BORE backport host models
========================

  python3 tools/testing/selftests/bore/test_backport.py --cc clang

Requires Python 3 and a host C compiler. HOSTCC is supported; default is cc.
The runner extracts production burst math/smoothing/scoring, priority tables,
load-weight setup, reweight helpers and inheritance/cache functions. It builds
and runs group-on and group-off models in a temporary directory. Assertions
are explicitly enabled even if NDEBUG is supplied.

Each model covers 104,996 math/weight cases, plus cache/inheritance cases:
* binary smoothing for all valid old/new penalties and smoothing settings;
* log/penalty boundaries, saturation, nice/score combinations and idle policy;
* queued/off-rq reweight accounting and old-weight PELT callback ordering;
* direct/topological averages, cache expiry and weighted subtree reuse;
* a 3,072-node chain/wide tree, shared traversal budget and one depth snapshot
  even when the simulated sysctl changes during that read.

Negative controls (both MUST fail with C assertions):

  python3 tools/testing/selftests/bore/test_backport.py --cc clang --donor-idle
  python3 tools/testing/selftests/bore/test_backport.py --cc clang --legacy-nice

The model's locks, lists, clocks, runqueues and PELT callbacks are shims. It
cannot test actual concurrency, PELT integration, update_curr re-entry, CFS
bandwidth throttling, scheduler migration, cpufreq, CPU hotplug, fairness or
responsiveness. Source guards and kernel object builds supplement it but do
not replace running-kernel stress tests. model.c is a runner template, not a
standalone kernel selftest binary or a runtime scheduler interface.

See Documentation/scheduler/bore-backport.rst for provenance, deviations,
configuration, compile coverage and important runtime validation limits.
